    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/tailapa-ii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tailapa-ii.jpg" alt="Tailapa II" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/tailapa-ii">Tailapa II</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Old_Kannada_inscription_dated_Shaka_913_(c.991_AD)_of_Kalyani_(Western)_Chalukya_King_Ahvamalla_Tailapa_II.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jugaari cross, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Western Chalukya Empire, also called the empire of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukyas</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basavakalyan">Kalyani</a> or the Later Chalukyas, was founded in 973 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tailapa-ii">Tailapa II</a>, a Rashtrakuta feudatory who governed lands around Tardavadi in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a> region. Tailapa claimed descent from the Chalukyas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badami">Badami</a>, whose empire the Rashtrakutas had overthrown in the eighth century, and presented his rise as the restoration of an ancient line, though the genealogical link is uncertain. He took advantage of the sack of the Rashtrakuta capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manyakheta">Manyakheta</a> by the Paramara <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siyaka">Siyaka II</a> in 972, overthrew the last Rashtrakuta king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karka-ii">Karka II</a>, and then defeated the loyalist resistance led by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-ganga-dynasty">Western Ganga</a> ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marasimha-ii">Marasimha II</a>, whose candidate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indra-iv">Indra IV</a> died in 982. Within a decade Tailapa had brought most of the old Rashtrakuta lands between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narmada-river">Narmada</a> and the Kaveri under his authority, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lata-region">Lata</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> for a time. His most famous victory came in the 990s against the Paramara king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vakpati-munja">Vakpati Munja</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a>, who invaded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>, was captured and, according to later tradition, executed. Tailapa ruled first from Manyakheta, and the dynasty later moved its seat to Kalyani, modern Basavakalyan in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bidar">Bidar</a> district, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/someshvara-i">Someshvara I</a>. Over two centuries his successors made the Deccan the centre of a long rivalry with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola Empire</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/lakkundi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lakkundi.jpg" alt="Lakkundi" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/lakkundi">Lakkundi</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:11th_century_Kasivisvesvara_temple,_Lakkundi,_Karnataka_India_-_03.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">P. Madhusudan, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The empire covered the black-soil plateau of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> and the fertile valleys of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tungabhadra-river">Tungabhadra</a>, where tanks and canals watered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugarcane">sugarcane</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> alongside dry crops of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">millet</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pulses">pulses</a>. Its prosperity rested on agriculture and on the activity of powerful merchant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a>. The best known, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ainnurruvar">Ainnurruvar</a> or Five Hundred Lords of Ayyavole, based at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aihole">Aihole</a>, traded across peninsular <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and overseas to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>, and its inscriptions record privileges granted by kings and local lords. Towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lakkundi">Lakkundi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balligave">Balligave</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basavakalyan">Kalyani</a> grew as centres of trade, minting and religion. Religious life was plural. Shaiva sects, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalamukha">Kalamukha</a> ascetics who ran large temple colleges at Balligave and elsewhere, enjoyed strong royal patronage; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jainism</a> remained influential, especially among merchants and noblewomen such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attimabbe">Attimabbe</a>, who sponsored copies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ponna">Ponna</a>’s works and a Jain temple at Lakkundi; and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaishnavism">Vaishnavism</a> grew. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> settlements called <em>agraharas</em> and temple <em>mathas</em> served as schools for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> learning, logic and law. Society was organised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> and occupation, with village assemblies, landholders and guilds playing an active role in local life, as thousands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> inscriptions on temple walls and stone tablets record gifts, disputes and public works.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/rajendra-chola-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rajendra-chola-i.jpg" alt="Rajendra Chola I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/rajendra-chola-i">Rajendra Chola I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rajendra_Chola_(cropped).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">David George, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>War with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola Empire</a> for the fertile <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raichur">Raichur</a> doab between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tungabhadra-river">Tungabhadra</a> and for influence over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vengi">Vengi</a> in coastal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andhra-region">Andhra</a> dominated the empire’s history. Around 1007 the Chola prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajendra-chola-i">Rajendra</a> invaded and ravaged the region around Donur, as the Hottur inscription records, and c. 1020 he defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayasimha-ii">Jayasimha II</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-maski">Maski</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/someshvara-i">Someshvara I</a> renewed the contest and at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-koppam">Battle of Koppam</a> in 1054 the Chola king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajadhiraja-chola">Rajadhiraja I</a> was killed, although his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajendra-chola-ii">Rajendra II</a> rallied the army and both sides claimed victory. Someshvara’s forces were defeated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kudalasangamam">Kudalasangamam</a> in the 1060s by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virarajendra-chola">Virarajendra Chola</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikramaditya-vi">Vikramaditya VI</a> was more successful: he exploited the troubles of the Chola king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kulottunga-i">Kulottunga I</a> to occupy Vengi c. 1118, and his generals contained the rising power of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoysala-empire">Hoysalas</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnuvardhana">Vishnuvardhana</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukyas</a> also fought the Paramaras of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a>, the Kalachuris of Tripuri and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaulukya-dynasty">Chaulukyas of Gujarat</a> in the north. Armies were raised largely by the great feudatories, the <em>mahamandaleshvaras</em>, who supplied infantry, cavalry and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">war elephants</a>, and imported horses from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> through the west-coast ports. This reliance on feudal levies gave the empire considerable strength in its prime but left the throne vulnerable when ambitious <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> turned their troops against it.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/vikramaditya-vi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vikramaditya-vi.jpg" alt="Vikramaditya VI" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/vikramaditya-vi">Vikramaditya VI</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Old_Kannada_inscription_(1077_AD)_of_Kalyani_Chalukya_King_Tribhuvanamalla_Vikramaditya_VI_in_Panchabasti_temple_at_Humcha.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">B. Lewis Rice (Benjamin Lewis Rice, 1837-1927), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The king ruled as <em>maharajadhiraja</em> from a court that combined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> learning with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> administration, and princes were often sent as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">viceroys</a> to frontier regions; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikramaditya-vi">Vikramaditya VI</a> himself governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banavasi">Banavasi</a> before taking the throne. The realm was divided into large numbered territorial units, such as the Banavasi Twelve Thousand, the Belvola Three Hundred and the Nolambavadi Thirty-two Thousand, whose figures reflected traditional reckonings of villages or revenue, and these were subdivided into smaller districts and villages run by headmen called <em>gavundas</em> and local assemblies. Much territory was held by hereditary feudatory families, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoysala-empire">Hoysalas</a>, the Kadambas of Hangal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goa">Goa</a>, the Silaharas of Kolhapur, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seuna-yadava-dynasty">Seuna Yadavas</a> and the Kakatiyas, who governed their lands, collected revenue and sent troops in return for royal recognition. Vikramaditya VI, who ruled from 1076 to 1126 after deposing his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/someshvara-ii">Someshvara II</a>, introduced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-vikrama-era">Chalukya-Vikrama era</a> to date records from his accession and presided over a court that included the Kashmiri poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bilhana">Bilhana</a>, author of the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikramankadevacharita">Vikramankadevacharita</a></em>, and the jurist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijnaneshvara">Vijnaneshvara</a>, whose <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitakshara">Mitakshara</a></em> commentary on the law code of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yajnavalkya">Yajnavalkya</a> became a leading authority on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> inheritance law. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/someshvara-iii">Someshvara III</a> compiled the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manasollasa">Manasollasa</a></em> in 1129, an encyclopaedia of royal life and government.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/kasivisvesvara-temple-lakkundi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kasivisvesvara-temple-lakkundi.jpg" alt="Kasivisvesvara Temple, Lakkundi" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/kasivisvesvara-temple-lakkundi">Kasivisvesvara Temple, Lakkundi</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:11th_century_Kasivisvesvara_temple,_Lakkundi,_Karnataka_India_-_12.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">P. Madhusudan, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Western Chalukyas sustained a Deccan empire that balanced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola Empire</a> for nearly two centuries, and their long rivalry shaped the political map of peninsular <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Their system of great feudatories nurtured the dynasties that succeeded them: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoysala-empire">Hoysalas</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seuna-yadava-dynasty">Seuna Yadavas</a> and the Kakatiyas all rose as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukya</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>, and their administration and courtly culture carried Chalukya forms into the thirteenth century. The period was a high point of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> literature. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranna">Ranna</a>, court poet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tailapa-ii">Tailapa II</a> and Satyashraya, wrote the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gadayuddha">Gadayuddha</a></em> and the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajitapurana">Ajitapurana</a></em>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durgasimha">Durgasimha</a> produced a Kannada <em>Panchatantra</em> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayasimha-ii">Jayasimha II</a>, and grammarians and prosodists such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagavarma-ii">Nagavarma II</a> codified the language. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> works of the court, especially the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitakshara">Mitakshara</a></em> and the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manasollasa">Manasollasa</a></em>, had lasting authority; the <em>Mitakshara</em> remained the main source of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> inheritance law in much of India into the twentieth century. In architecture the Western Chalukyas developed a richly ornamented style in soapstone and fine-grained stone, with stepped plans, lathe-turned pillars and dense surface carving, seen in the Mahadeva temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/itagi">Itagi</a>, praised in its inscription as an emperor among temples, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kasivisvesvara-temple-lakkundi">Kasivishveshvara</a> temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lakkundi">Lakkundi</a> and the temples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gadag">Gadag</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dambal">Dambal</a>. This style led directly to the better-known Hoysala temples.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/bijjala-ii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bijjala-ii.jpg" alt="Bijjala II" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/bijjala-ii">Bijjala II</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kalachuris_of_Kalyana._temp_Bijjala.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CNG Coins, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The empire’s decline in the twelfth century came from within. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikramaditya-vi">Vikramaditya VI</a>, his successors faced growing independence among the great feudatories, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a> pressure on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vengi">Vengi</a> resumed and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoysala-empire">Hoysalas</a> expanded northwards. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tailapa-iii">Tailapa III</a>, who reigned from 1151, the Kakatiya chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prola-ii">Prola II</a> defeated and captured the king, and the Kalachuri feudatory <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijjala-ii">Bijjala II</a>, commander of the royal army, took control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basavakalyan">Kalyani</a> c. 1157 and assumed full royal titles in 1162. During Kalachuri rule the minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basava">Basava</a> led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lingayatism">Lingayat</a> or Virashaiva movement, whose devotional <em>vachana</em> poetry became one of the great bodies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> literature. The Kalachuris themselves were weakened by Bijjala’s assassination c. 1167 and by unrest among their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>, and in 1184 Tailapa III’s son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/someshvara-iv">Someshvara IV</a>, aided by the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahma">Brahma</a>, restored the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukya dynasty</a> at Kalyani. The restoration lasted only a few years. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seuna-yadava-dynasty">Seuna Yadava</a> ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhillama-v">Bhillama V</a> took the northern provinces and Kalyani, while the Hoysala <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veera-ballala-ii">Veera Ballala II</a> defeated the Chalukya forces in the south, and Someshvara IV disappears from the records c. 1189. The empire was divided between the Seuna Yadavas of Devagiri and the Hoysalas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dwarasamudra">Dwarasamudra</a>, with the Kakatiyas holding the east, and the Chalukya name passed out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> politics.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Western Chalukya Empire restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukya</a> rule over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> in 973 and contested south <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Cholas</a> for two centuries, while its courts produced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitakshara">Mitakshara</a>, a golden age of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> literature and an ornate temple style, and its feudatories grew into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoysala-empire">Hoysala</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seuna-yadava-dynasty">Yadava</a> and Kakatiya kingdoms.</em></p>

